00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:15.590
Air travel is back… with an asterisk.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:24.730
In 2019, 79,332,046 people passed through
TSA checkpoints in the month of July.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.410
In 2021, 63,118,017 did—representing an
80% demand recovery.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:35.230
This blows away most expert forecasts from
2020, and is a remarkable number considering

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.670
international travel, and certainly international
tourism, is still nearly nonexistent.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:45.629
In Europe, air traffic has recovered to around
70% of 2019 levels.

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:50.649
In China, the number of scheduled seats has
already surpassed 2019 numbers—they’re

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:52.739
beyond 100% recovery.

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:58.289
So, the US, Europe, and China—the world’s
three largest aviation markets—have experienced

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:01.719
recoveries beyond the most bullish expectations.

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:04.210
But now, the asterisk.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:10.990
In the second financial quarter of 2019, United
Airlines earned $11,402,000,000 in operating

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:11.990
revenue.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:20.689
In the same quarter of 2021, however, just
$5,471,000,000—about 48% of the 2019 number.

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:28.110
Meanwhile, American and Delta’s second quarter
2021 revenues were 62.5% and 56.8%, respectively,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.159
compared to 2019.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:35.200
Context does not make these numbers better,
because in the second quarter of 2021, about

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.229
67% as many travelers passed through TSA checkpoints
as 2019.

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:44.530
Simply put, revenue recovery is lagging behind
demand recovery.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.680
A culprit is easily identifiable.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:53.939
In the second quarter of 2019, United earned
14.32 cents per seat, per mile flown—“available

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:55.361
seat mile” as its called in the industry.

00:01:55.361 --> 00:02:05.460
In 2021, they’re earning just 11.02 cents
per ASM—76.95% of that of 2019.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:14.180
Meanwhile, American’s revenue per ASM is
at about 78.84% of 2019, while Delta’s is

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.180
69.45%.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.870
While overall revenue should be considered
entirely within the context of overall demand,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.670
revenue per ASM should not be.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.470
Airlines decide how many seat miles to make
available, they decide how many flights to

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:32.290
fly, so the fact that airlines are earning
some 20-30% less per seat stamps a massive

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.510
asterisk on the triumphant calls of travel’s
return.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.500
Travelers may be back, but they’re not the
same.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.430
In the US’ summer 2021, the entire country
seemingly went on vacation.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:49.080
Hilton’s in Hawaii were going for Four Seasons
prices, rental cars in Alaska were a luxury

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:54.180
afforded to only the most foresighted travelers,
and Ubers in Vegas were as elusive as a royal

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.180
flush.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:01.760
The US’ tourism infrastructure was at absolute
peak capacity in summer 2021, but then there’s

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:08.180
this: Midtown Manhattan, DC’s K Street,
Chicago’s Loop—America’s major business

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.780
districts still sit abandoned.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:16.190
Despite the resurgence in life in the US,
many of the country’s largest office-based

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:21.319
employers, including Google, Microsoft, Apple,
Facebook, Deloitte, Cisco, and McKinsey, have

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:24.700
yet to return to the office in any meaningful
way.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:29.280
When companies of this nature travel, they’re
doing so to visit other companies so, without

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.980
open offices, there are no companies to visit.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:37.050
Therefore, the fact these these companies,
which include the top corporate travel spenders

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.830
in the country, have yet to return to the
office explains why business travel has yet

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.750
to return meaningfully.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:50.120
For United, American, Delta, British Airways,
Air France, Cathay Pacific, Singapore Airlines,

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.900
Qantas—all the old, storied international
airlines that you’ve heard of—this represents

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.629
a disproportionately dramatic problem.

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:03.209
These airlines—full-service network carriers,
as they’re called—are essentially designed

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.750
to cater to the needs of business travelers.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:10.500
Take, for example, the difference between
Southwest, a leisure-focused airline, and

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.950
United, a business-focused airline.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:17.479
If, for example, someone from Phoenix was
travelling to Bozeman, Montana to visit Yellowstone

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:21.160
National Park, they could choose to fly on
Southwest.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.320
During the summer season, this airline operates
a nonstop flight on the route each Saturday

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.800
and Sunday morning.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.400
The same traveller could also choose to fly
on United Airlines, through either Denver

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:37.720
or Los Angeles, leaving at 6:10, 9:40, 11:48,
1:55, or 3:41 pm each day.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.840
Leisure travelers, however, disproportionately
value the convenience of a nonstop flight

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.620
and they wouldn’t mind organizing their
trip around the two days per week when a flight

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.240
operates.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:49.190
Business travelers, however, are different.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.900
Let’s say a sales rep from Phoenix needed
to travel to a client meeting in Memphis,

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.560
taking place from 2:00 to 6:00 pm on a Tuesday.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.410
Southwest does have a nonstop flight between
the cities, but in order to make that meeting,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:06.340
the traveller would have to take the Monday
flight, leaving at 12:25 pm, arriving at 5:20

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:07.340
pm.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.199
They’d then have to sit around in Memphis
for 21 hours until the meeting, then wait

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:15.400
another 23 hours after for the 4:00 pm Wednesday
flight back.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:19.840
On United, however, the same traveller could
leave at 6:00 am on Tuesday, connect through

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.020
Denver, arrive at 1:00 pm, make the 2:00 pm
meeting, then take a 7:00 am flight back through

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.870
Houston the following morning and arrive at
the office by noon, ready for the rest of

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.330
the day’s work.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:34.189
Faced with the prospect of waiting around
in Memphis for almost a day more, they’ll

00:05:34.189 --> 00:05:36.639
almost always pick United.

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:41.850
In all, business travelers value not only
the day of travel, but the time of day of

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:42.850
travel.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.630
Therefore, it’s all a compromise—do you
want flights going exactly where you want

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:51.039
them to, or flights leaving exactly when you
want them to?

00:05:51.039 --> 00:05:55.520
Disproportionately, leisure travelers value
the former, business travelers the latter.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.930
This is why nearly every United, Delta, and
American flight routes through one of their

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:04.400
hubs—by consolidating travelers onto more
regular flights to fewer destinations, they

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.020
can offer far more choices of flight itineraries.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.500
The optimization for business travel extends
beyond this to full service network carriers’

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:17.139
focus on premium cabins, frequent flyer programs,
airport services, and more, but fundamentally,

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:21.780
there are stark differences between airlines
made for leisure travelers and those for business

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:26.520
travelers, and many of the world’s largest
airlines fall into that second category.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:30.110
Historically, however, it’s been worth it.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:33.700
Business travelers are on the road year round,
rather than when the weather is good or school

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:38.210
is out, meaning they represent a more reliable
client base for airlines.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:43.740
In addition, they’re more price inelastic—that’s
to say, when a consultant needs to travel,

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.490
they truly need to travel, and will book a
ticket regardless of the price.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.470
While leisure travelers will make decisions
on price, businesses will pay whatever it

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:56.520
takes to get their staff where they need to
go to make them money.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.020
Only about 20% of domestic trips are taken
by those on business, but they account for

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.100
far more than 20% of revenue.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.259
That’s because of what’s referred to as
price discrimination.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:12.139
It works off of a simple truth: leisure travelers
overwhelmingly make their plans and book trips

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:17.289
months in advance, while business travelers
book weeks or days in advance.

00:07:17.289 --> 00:07:21.300
Based on their own financial disclosures,
we know that the average United flight length

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:27.099
is currently 1,460 miles or 2,350 kilometers,
and that their average cost to operate a flight

00:07:27.099 --> 00:07:31.819
of that length, per passenger, is $199.58.

00:07:31.819 --> 00:07:36.229
We also know that, two months before, United
is selling tickets for eight of these twelve

00:07:36.229 --> 00:07:42.400
roughly 1,460 mile flights for below what
it costs to operate on a per-passenger basis.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.090
In fact, the overall average fare for these
twelve flights sits below their operating

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:48.620
cost two months before departure.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:53.160
While the sale of ancillaries such as extra
legroom seats, checked luggage, wifi, food

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:57.250
and beverage, and more would likely push them
above break-even, it’s clear that United

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:02.400
would not turn a significant profit on the
prices most of us pay for tickets, but that’s

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:03.729
by design.

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:07.770
To fill planes, they need to sell cheap tickets,
at least at first.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:12.350
In the weeks leading up to a flight, however,
fares rise dramatically, so by the day before

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:17.030
departure, the average fare for those twelve
flights more than doubles to $413.83—these

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:22.910
are the fares businesses are paying for their
typically last-minute travel.

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:28.290
Now, on that average 1,460 mile flight, imagine
for the sake of simplicity that it was operated

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:31.620
by an all-economy, 179 seat 737-900.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:37.050
We’d know based on United’s company-wide
load factor that 150 of its seats should be

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.050
filled.

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:42.860
In this case, their operating cost would total
$29,937.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:48.440
Their total revenue from that flight, however,
would be $36,245.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.570
That means the operating profit margin is
$6,308.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:57.269
Imagining again for the sake of simplicity
that United sold this hypothetical, perfectly

00:08:57.269 --> 00:09:05.480
average flight for either that $199.58 or
$413.83 price point, that would mean, to attain

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:12.170
their average $36,245 in revenue, they would
need to sell 120 of the cheaper tickets, largely

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:15.950
to just leisure travelers, and a mere 30 at
the higher price point.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:21.700
That’s just 20% of the overall tickets sold
on this flight but 20% happens to represent

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:27.310
another important statistic: the average proportion
of domestic air travel in the US completed

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:28.650
by business travelers.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:33.170
It’s not surprising that these numbers work
together so perfectly, because this is the

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:37.920
exact equation United and other business-oriented
airlines have been using for decades.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:44.120
In the case of this hypothetical flight, its
the final 15 passengers, half of the business

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.080
travelers, that make up the entirety of the
airline’s profit.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.390
Now, however, in the COVID era, they’re
gone.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:57.930
Southwest Airlines averaged 10.22 cents in
operating costs per available seat mile in

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:03.971
2021’s second quarter, while United averaged
14.49 cents—a dramatical difference, simply

00:10:03.971 --> 00:10:07.870
explained by the fact that Southwest is a
low-cost airline, meaning its operating costs

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.180
are low, and United is not.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:15.750
In a business environment now devoid of the
majority of price inelastic business travelers,

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:20.850
airlines like United have and will have a
lot harder time keeping up with the recovery.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:25.579
While United, Delta, and American’s revenue
recovery all fell behind demand recovery in

00:10:25.579 --> 00:10:28.820
the second quarter, Southwest’s was on-track.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:36.170
67% overall demand recovery was, in their
case, accompanied by 67.83% revenue recovery.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:42.700
And now, as August turns into September, recovery
is only going to get tougher.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:48.470
Summer 2021 was one of the busiest on record
for many American and European tourist destinations,

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.460
but that’s coming to an end.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:57.060
In a normal year, after a summer lull counterbalanced
by the busiest leisure travel season, business

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:01.339
travel starts picking up come early September
and stays above average through the end of

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:02.339
the year.

00:11:02.339 --> 00:11:06.630
This helps stabilize demand during a season
when few travel for leisure purposes, but

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:10.990
this year, that’s not going to be there
in its full extent.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:14.920
This abnormally one-sided transition is already
happening.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:20.100
Travel demand in the US dropped 2.8% between
the week of July 25th and August 1st, then

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:22.480
another 3% the week of August 8th.

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:27.570
It’s a small drop, but there are some initial
indications that it’s happening faster than

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:32.649
in 2019, when the first eighteen days of August
saw 4.2% fewer travelers than the last eighteen

00:11:32.649 --> 00:11:34.170
days of July.

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:40.040
This year, the difference between those two
sets of days is 5.5%.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:44.410
In absolute terms that might not seem like
a dramatic difference, but in relative terms

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:49.450
it means the demand drop is happening 30%
faster, and these weeks represent only the

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:53.769
very first of leisure travel’s end of summer
retreat.

00:11:53.769 --> 00:11:58.431
This trend will likely continue, and, more
importantly, accelerate as leisure travel

00:11:58.431 --> 00:12:02.040
continues to drop, and business travel fails
to rise.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:07.310
In the US, the next strong leisure travel
period doesn’t come until Thanksgiving,

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:10.529
in November, and in Europe, it’s Christmas.

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:14.920
And of course, the Delta variant’s spread
surely will not help, as businesses will play

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:19.399
it safe with office openings and corporate
travel—now realizing that the path out of

00:12:19.399 --> 00:12:22.730
the pandemic is not linear in nature.

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:26.740
For airlines, there’s not a whole lot that
can be done at this point.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.890
What the world lacks right now is confidence.

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:34.260
In the US, the University of Michigan’s
consumer sentiment index, tracking confidence

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:38.940
in the overall state in the economy, recorded
one of its largest drops in history between

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:41.529
July and August 2021.

00:12:41.529 --> 00:12:46.370
It reached a level of 70.2, which is lower
than any point in the pandemic so far, and

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:49.360
its lowest level since the great recession.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:53.959
This is reflective of the amount of uncertainty
the Delta variant and its rise in a post-vaccine

00:12:53.959 --> 00:12:58.540
era has caused, despite the fact that in the
US, Europe, the UK, and other places where

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:03.370
vaccines are now widely available, hospitalizations
and deaths are still far from where they were

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:05.839
when consumer sentiment was higher.

00:13:05.839 --> 00:13:10.140
Without the certainty of whether the pandemic
will worsen or improve in the coming months,

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:14.751
however, businesses will not make the big
decisions that will bring business travel

00:13:14.751 --> 00:13:16.200
back.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:22.200
Summer will have likely marked the first profitable
period of the pandemic for European and American

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.870
airlines, but it seems increasingly unlikely
that the recovery will continue its upward

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:29.190
trend in fall without strong business travel.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:34.110
There’s also the question of whether business
travel will ever fully recover—most experts

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:38.540
believe doomsday predictions of Zoom killing
business travel to be overblown, however,

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:43.300
there is a realistic possibility that 2019
could serve as the historical high-water mark

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:47.620
for corporate travel as the world of big business
moves increasingly digital, and concerns about

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:51.970
the environmental impact of flying to lunch
in another city mount.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:57.459
If this proves true, subtle changes will occur
in the coming years that make United and Lufthansa

00:13:57.459 --> 00:14:00.230
look more like Southwest and EasyJet.

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:05.769
COVID should have taught airlines just how
important flexibility and adaptability is.

00:14:05.769 --> 00:14:10.579
It’s hard to fault airlines for failing
to turn a profit during the depths of a crisis,

00:14:10.579 --> 00:14:16.029
but now, as much of the developed world plots
a slow, bumpy path out of the pandemic, there

00:14:16.029 --> 00:14:19.079
is opportunity for profitability.

00:14:19.079 --> 00:14:23.589
Therefore, this is when the test truly begins.

00:14:23.589 --> 00:14:28.459
This is what airlines were waiting for so
now, with cash reserves wained and investor

00:14:28.459 --> 00:14:35.940
goodwill gone, the coming months are truly
make or break for airlines.

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:40.470
Last year, around when COVID was starting,
